  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates upper level ridging transitioning east through \nthe Western High Plains. Near the surface, a lee side trough of low \npressure remains anchored in extreme southeast Colorado.\n\nRelatively dry conditions are expected to persist early in the \nperiod with the SREF indicating an upper level ridge transitioning \nslowly east into the Central Plains Thursday, giving way to a \nsouthwesterly flow aloft across the Western High Plains. The overall \npattern becomes more active Thursday evening into early Friday as a \nsouthwesterly flow aloft slowly develops in wake of departing ridge\naloft. Near the surface, a developing low in southeast Colorado this\nevening is projected to push slowly into southwest Kansas early\nThursday morning ahead of an approaching weak cold front that is\nexpected to stall out somewhere across southwest/central Kansas\nby Thursday afternoon. Meanwhile, a return flow will enhance \nmoisture advection into central Kansas and wrap around the low \ninto western Kansas, pushing surface dewpoints well up into the \n60s(F) and providing ample instability. This will set the stage \nfor thunderstorm development late afternoon/early evening as H5 \nvort maxima associated with an upper level shortwave lifts \nnortheast out of the Desert Southwest into the Western High \nPlains, interacting with the aforementioned stalled boundary. \nThe latest HREF suggests the best chance for thunderstorms to be\nacross west central Kansas into extreme southwest Kansas where \nthere is a 20-40% probability of 6-hr QPF exceeding 0.25 of an \ninch by late Thursday evening. A similar setup is forecast \nFriday with little movement to the stalled boundary expected.\n\nUnseasonably warm temperatures are forecast again tonight for south \ncentral Kansas and portions of southwest Kansas, but a weak cold \nfront moving in from the northwest may help drop temperatures back \nnear seasonal levels in west central Kansas and extreme southwest \nKansas. This is supported by the HREF showing a 70-90% probability \nof temperatures slipping below 70F in our west/northwest. Lows \ngenerally down into the 70s(F) can be expected elsewhere. The front \nwill also have an effect on afternoon highs out west where the HREF \npaints a 20-40% probability of temperatures exceeding 90F while \npointing to a 40-60% probability of topping 100F in south central \nKansas. A similar temperature range is expected Friday with little \nmovement to the stalled boundary.",
